</code></pre>
<p>If you look under the hood, you may notice the following exceptions (which cannot be caught using a simple try/catch) in the <code>quiet</code> zone:</p>
<p>SocketException: Connection attempt cancelled, host: rss.nytimes.com, port: 443</p>
<p>ClientException with SocketException: Connection attempt cancelled, host: rss.nytimes.com, port: 443, uri=https://rss.nytimes.com/services/xml/rss/nyt/Sports.xml</p>
<p>ClientException with SocketException: Connection attempt cancelled, host: rss.nytimes.com, port: 443, uri=https://rss.nytimes.com/services/xml/rss/nyt/Science.xml</p>
<p>All these errors are created due to intentional (on request) closing of the client.<br />
Thus, these exceptions are expected and <code>it would be strange if they were not thrown</code>.<br />
All you need to do is <code>replace it with another exception</code>.<br />
In the above example, this exception is <code>TaskCanceledError</code>.<br />
So, everything is logical; we <code>replace these expected exceptions</code> with <code>something else</code> (in accordance with the logic of the operation).</p>

Q: Download Image with url React A: <p>To download the image in react create a new download button component and pass the image path as a prop to that component. You will have to do something similar to the this:-</p>
<pre><code>  import React, { useState } from 'react';
interface DownloadButtonProps{
path:string
}
const DownloadButton&lt;DownloadButtonProps&gt; = (props) =&gt; {
  const [imageUrl] = useState(props.path); // Replace with the actual path to your image

  const handleDownloadClick = () =&gt; {
    // Create a Blob object for the image
    fetch(imageUrl)
      .then((response) =&gt; response.blob())
      .then((blob) =&gt; {
        // Create a URL for the Blob
        const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);

        // Create an anchor element for downloading
        const a = document.createElement('a');
        a.href = url;
        a.download = 'downloaded-image.jpg'; // Specify the desired download filename

        // Programmatically trigger a click event on the anchor element
        a.click();

        // Clean up by revoking the URL
        URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
      });
  };

  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;button onClick={handleDownloadClick}&gt;Download Image&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
};

export default DownloadButton;
</code></pre>

